𝟑𝟎 𝐃𝐚𝐲𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 – 𝐃𝐚𝐲 𝟏𝟕/𝟑𝟎

🔥 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐓𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐬 – 𝐌𝐚𝐤𝐞 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐟𝐢𝐠𝐮𝐫𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐃𝐲𝐧𝐚𝐦𝐢𝐜

When managing multiple servers and environments, maintaining separate configuration files can quickly become difficult.

This is where 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐓𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐬 become extremely useful in Ansible. 🚀

Instead of hardcoding values:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;
}

We can create a dynamic .j2 template:

server {
    listen {{ nginx_port }};
    server_name {{ server_name }};
    root {{ document_root }};
}

Ansible replaces these variables with the appropriate values during execution.

⚙️ 𝐔𝐬𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐭𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞 𝐌𝐨𝐝𝐮𝐥𝐞

- name: Deploy Nginx configuration
  template:
    src: nginx.conf.j2
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  notify: Restart Nginx

📌 src → Jinja2 template
📌 dest → Target configuration file
📌 {{ variable }} → Dynamic value

🌍 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

Imagine you have:

🔹 Development
🔹 QA
🔹 Staging
🔹 Production

Each environment may have different ports, URLs, paths, or other configuration values.

Instead of maintaining multiple configuration files:

❌ Duplicate files
❌ Manual changes
❌ Higher chance of mistakes

With Jinja2:

✅ One reusable template
✅ Environment-specific variables
✅ Consistent configuration
✅ Automated deployment

🔥 𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐂𝐚𝐧 𝐃𝐨 𝐌𝐨𝐫𝐞

Jinja2 supports:

✅ Variables
✅ Conditions
✅ Loops
✅ Filters
✅ Expressions

⚠️ 𝐂𝐨𝐩𝐲 𝐯𝐬 𝐓𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞

𝐜𝐨𝐩𝐲 → Static files
𝐭𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐞 → Dynamic files

🎯 𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐓𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐚𝐰𝐚𝐲

𝐉𝐢𝐧𝐣𝐚𝟐 𝐡𝐞𝐥𝐩𝐬 𝐮𝐬 𝐦𝐨𝐯𝐞 𝐟𝐫𝐨𝐦 𝐦𝐚𝐧𝐮𝐚𝐥 𝐜𝐨𝐧𝐟𝐢𝐠𝐮𝐫𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐭𝐨 𝐝𝐲𝐧𝐚𝐦𝐢𝐜, 𝐫𝐞𝐮𝐬𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐚𝐮𝐭𝐨𝐦𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐝 𝐜𝐨𝐧𝐟𝐢𝐠𝐮𝐫𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧.
